SECTION 16 — QUALITY AND CONSISTENCY RULES

45. Mandatory quality standards

Never:

- Contradict Canon.md.
- Invent dates that break the timeline.
- Make every event supernatural.
- Make every ancient civilization technologically superior.
- Make every important figure secretly divine.
- Connect every mystery to one central conspiracy.
- Make every kingdom culturally identical.
- Create endless world-ending catastrophes.
- Treat all historical accounts as equally reliable.
- Confuse legend with established fact.
- Give ancient civilizations knowledge they could not reasonably possess.
- Ignore the consequences of wars.
- Forget the ordinary population.
- Introduce a historical civilization without explaining its legacy.
- Write future plot events as completed history.

Always:

- Check chronology.
- Explain causality.
- Preserve uncertainty.
- Establish cultural differences.
- Consider geography.
- Consider economics.
- Consider population.
- Consider historical sources.
- Track long-term consequences.
- Cross-reference existing documentation.
- Mark proposed lore.
- Maintain the established endpoint.

